  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how organic electrode materials could be used in lithium batteries and how their properties translate into real battery performance. The top portion compares three cell configurations. In two “charge first” designs, either an n-type material serves as the cathode with a lithium-containing cathode partner, or a p-type material serves as the anode with a lithium-containing anode partner. Arrows indicate electron flow through the outer circuit and lithium-ion movement between electrodes, while the molecular sketches suggest reversible redox reactions at the organic materials. A third configuration is labeled “discharge first,” where a lithiated organic n-type anode is paired with a neutral n-type cathode, again showing lithium shuttling and electron transfer during operation.\n\nThe lower portion shifts from chemistry to application. It presents a progression from material-level parameters—such as energy density, power density, cycle life, density, conductivity, efficiency, cost, resource availability, and thermal or chemical stability—to the practical battery metrics that matter most: energy density, power density, cycle life, cost, safety, and recyclability. Overall, the figure links molecular redox design and electrode pairing strategies with broader performance goals for usable battery systems.",
